# AI/ML Development Guidelines

Apply these rules when working with LangChain, LangGraph, and AI model integrations.

## Model Providers

### Supported Providers
- **OpenAI**: GPT-4, GPT-4o, GPT-4o-mini via `@langchain/openai`
- **Azure OpenAI**: Enterprise deployments via `@langchain/azure-openai`
- **Anthropic**: Claude models via `@langchain/anthropic`
- **Google**: Gemini models via `@langchain/google-genai`
- **Perplexity**: Online/search models via API

### Model Selection
- Use GPT-4o-mini or Claude Haiku for high-volume, simple tasks
- Use GPT-4o or Claude Sonnet for complex reasoning
- Use GPT-4 or Claude Opus for critical decisions requiring highest accuracy
- Consider cost vs. quality tradeoffs for each use case

### Provider Configuration
- Store API keys in environment variables, never in code
- Use model-agnostic abstractions where possible
- Implement fallback providers for resilience
- Set appropriate timeouts for each provider

## LangChain Patterns

### Chain Construction
- Use LCEL (LangChain Expression Language) for chain composition
- Prefer `RunnableSequence` over legacy `LLMChain`
- Use `RunnablePassthrough` for data forwarding
- Implement `RunnableLambda` for custom transformations

### Prompts
- Store prompts in dedicated template files or constants
- Use `ChatPromptTemplate.fromMessages()` for chat models
- Include system message with clear role definition
- Use few-shot examples for complex tasks
- Implement prompt versioning for production prompts

### Memory and Context
- Use `BufferMemory` for simple conversational context
- Implement `ConversationSummaryMemory` for long conversations
- Consider `VectorStoreRetrieverMemory` for semantic retrieval
- Clear memory appropriately between sessions

### Output Parsing
- Use `StructuredOutputParser` with Zod schemas for typed responses
- Implement `OutputFixingParser` for resilience
- Validate parsed outputs before use
- Handle parsing failures gracefully

## LangGraph Patterns

### Graph Construction
- Define clear state schemas with TypeScript interfaces
- Use descriptive node names that reflect their purpose
- Keep node functions focused and single-purpose
- Implement conditional edges for branching logic

### State Management
- Use immutable state updates
- Define clear state transitions
- Log state changes for debugging
- Implement checkpointing for long-running graphs

### Error Handling in Graphs
- Add error handling nodes for recovery
- Implement retry logic for transient failures
- Use fallback paths for graceful degradation
- Log graph execution traces

## Vector Stores and Embeddings

### Embedding Models
- Use `text-embedding-3-small` for cost efficiency
- Use `text-embedding-3-large` for higher quality
- Cache embeddings when content is static
- Batch embedding requests for efficiency

### Vector Store Operations
- Use appropriate similarity thresholds
- Implement metadata filtering for scoped searches
- Monitor index size and performance
- Clean up stale vectors periodically

## Streaming and Real-time

### Streaming Responses
- Use `streamEvents()` for granular control
- Implement proper SSE (Server-Sent Events) formatting
- Handle client disconnections gracefully
- Send heartbeats for long operations

### Token Management
- Track token usage for cost monitoring
- Implement token limits per request/user
- Use streaming to show progress for long responses
- Handle context window limits gracefully

## Safety and Quality

### Content Moderation
- Implement input validation before sending to models
- Filter or flag potentially harmful outputs
- Log moderation events for review
- Use model-specific safety settings where available

### Hallucination Mitigation
- Ground responses in retrieved context (RAG)
- Implement fact-checking for critical information
- Use lower temperature for factual tasks
- Provide source citations when possible

### Testing AI Components
- Mock LLM calls in unit tests
- Use deterministic seeds where available
- Test edge cases (empty input, long input, special characters)
- Implement evaluation metrics for quality

## Cost Optimization

- Cache identical requests
- Use cheaper models for preprocessing/filtering
- Implement request batching where appropriate
- Monitor and alert on unusual usage patterns
- Set spending limits per user/tenant
